From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:30:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: place and take calls through the Phone app over LS2

tdlib-purple already does the whole call - TDLib signalling and libtgvoip
media - but its UI is Pidgin's: a purple_request_action() accept dialog, a
"/hangup" command, and a gate on the front-end's PurpleMedia capabilities.
webOS has none of that. What it has is the stock Phone app, which drives
calls of other services through an LS2 contract (CallSynergizer), as it
does for the WhatsApp connector.

call-luna.{h,cpp} serves that contract as com.palm.telegram.call,
registered when the account connects, on libpurple's own GLib main loop:
callStateQuery (subscription), dial, answer, disconnect and hangupAll. They
reach TDLib through three bridge functions in call.cpp (callBridgeDial/
Answer/Hangup), and updateCall() pushes every state change - incoming,
dialing, active, disconnected with a cause (normal, rejected, missed,
error) - instead of writing to the conversation or opening a dialog. The
media-caps gate is bypassed, and the call's audio route is switched by
setting audiod's phone scenario while a call is active.
The accept/cancel dialog's callbacks (acceptCallCb, discardCallCb) have no
caller left and are removed; answering goes through acceptCurrentCall().

Dialing is made to cope with how the Phone app dials:

- an "id<n>" buddy whose user TDLib has not loaded yet (right after login)
  is called by id directly; createCall only needs the id.
- a bare "+E.164" number (dialpad, or a contact not linked to a Telegram
  buddy) is resolved with searchUserByPhoneNumber first, local contacts or
  not; if it is not on Telegram the call card is closed with an error
  instead of hanging on "Connecting".
- the dialer may send both the resolved "id<n>" and the raw number for one
  contact in the same tick. createCall is asynchronous, so hasActiveCall()
  is still false for the second; a synchronous "initiating" flag drops it,
  and its card is closed.
- an outgoing call is reported under the exact address that was dialed,
  so the plugin's card merges with the dialer's instead of leaving a
  second one stuck on "Connecting".

Video is plumbed through as far as the platform allows: a video call is
requested with createCall.is_video_, reported to the Phone app as such,
and on activation a clonk (webOS media server) video session is opened and
libtgvoip is given a VideoSource/VideoRenderer (voipkit-tgvoip.{h,cpp}) that
exchange H.264 access units with it. The bridge to the media server's RTP
sockets is behind voipkit.h; here it is voipkit_none.c, a null backend
that logs once and lets the call carry on as voice only, which is all
LuneOS can do (the legacy webOS backend is a separate patch). The
initial libtgvoip SetVideoSource() is retried for a few seconds, because
libtgvoip only creates its outgoing video stream once the peer's first
PKT_INIT has arrived.

callProtocol.library_versions_ is left empty on purpose: declaring "2.7.7"
makes current clients move to a newer signalling path the bundled
VoIPController cannot speak, and audio breaks with it.

Building needs luna-service2 (lunaservice_INCLUDE_DIRS/_LIBRARIES; the
include list must also carry webos-ls2-compat.h, which maps the legacy
split-bus API onto luna-service2 3.x) and C, for voipkit_none.c.
-Dtgvoip_NO_DSP=ON matches a libtgvoip built with --disable-dsp.

call-luna.cpp and call.cpp log the call life cycle to
/media/internal/tgcall.log, and libtgvoip is told to write its log and
stats to /media/internal/tgvoip.log and tgvoip-stats.log; purple_debug
output does not reach the system log on webOS.

Not for upstream: the LS2 service, the Phone app contract and the media
server are webOS only.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
